    {
      "id": "https://www.ire.org/?p=211051",
      "title": "In an era of rewritten history and disappearing data, archives are a lifeline for journalists",
      "link": "https://www.ire.org/2026/10/01/in-an-era-of-rewritten-history-and-disappearing-data-archives-are-a-lifeline-for-journalists/",
      "published": "Thu, 01 Oct 2026 18:43:56 +0000",
      "summary_raw": "<p>Greg Shea / GBH Archives By Rebecca Fraimow &#038; Peter Higgins, WGBH &#124; Published October 1, 2026 In 2008, GBH&#8217;s investigative news program Frontline was putting together their regular special on the year&#8217;s presidential candidates: The Choice, featuring Barack Obama and John McCain. Working on a hunch, the editors went back into GBH&#8217;s local news&#8230;</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.ire.org/2026/10/01/in-an-era-of-rewritten-history-and-disappearing-data-archives-are-a-lifeline-for-journalists/\">In an era of rewritten history and disappearing data, archives are a lifeline for journalists</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.ire.org\">Investigative Reporters and Editors</a>.</p>",
      "source": "IRE (Investigative Reporters & Editors)"
    },
    {
      "id": "https://wan-ifra.org/?p=121296",
      "title": "How The Washington Post is making relentless journalism visible",
      "link": "https://wan-ifra.org/2026/10/how-the-washington-post-is-making-relentless-journalism-visible/",
      "published": "Fri, 02 Oct 2026 08:55:37 +0000",
      "summary_raw": "<p>We keep saying it, and with good reason: news organisations must become much better at taking readers behind the byline. We need to show not only what journalism uncovers, but how it is made and what it takes to produce.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://wan-ifra.org/2026/10/how-the-washington-post-is-making-relentless-journalism-visible/\">How The Washington Post is making relentless journalism visible</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://wan-ifra.org\">WAN-IFRA</a>.</p>",
      "source": "WAN-IFRA"
    },
    {
      "id": "https://wan-ifra.org/?p=121127",
      "title": "The journalist as a modern day Robin Hood",
      "link": "https://wan-ifra.org/2026/10/the-journalist-as-a-modern-day-robin-hood/",
      "published": "Fri, 02 Oct 2026 08:52:37 +0000",
      "summary_raw": "<p>We are flooded with information, yet understanding the world has never felt harder. It is time for a new contract between journalists and the public, writes Amalie Kestler, editor in chief, Politiken.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://wan-ifra.org/2026/10/the-journalist-as-a-modern-day-robin-hood/\">The journalist as a modern day Robin Hood</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://wan-ifra.org\">WAN-IFRA</a>.</p>",
      "source": "WAN-IFRA"
    },
    {
      "id": "https://wan-ifra.org/?p=121304",
      "title": "‘A dawn of hope’: How Ethiopia’s Addis Maleda left print behind",
